  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 443 AM EDT SUN SEP 20 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Monday evening with fairly zonal flow\naloft. At the surface, a strong area of high pressure will be\nsituated south of James Bay. South of this high, a cold front \nwill be draped from the Mid-Atlantic across the Commonwealth, \ntrapped between the high pressure to the north and parallel upper-\nlevel steering winds. The result: showers and thunderstorms will \nlikely be ongoing Monday evening.\n\nFarther west, an upper-level shortwave and broad troughing west of\nthe Great Lakes will slowly translate eastward toward the Upper\nMidwest and Mississippi Valley. Quasi-zonal flow ahead of the \nshortwave, coupled with multiple perturbations riding along the \nstalled front, will allow for numerous to widespread showers and \nisolated to scattered thunderstorms through Wednesday. WPC has \nplaced portions of the CWA in a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) ERO \nTuesday and Wednesday due to some potential for excessive \nrainfall. Areas that experience repeated rounds of storms could \nsee isolated instances of flash flooding.\n\nOn Thursday, amplification and passage of the upper-level trough\nshould bring an end to the prolonged wet pattern. As the trough\naxis moves east of the area, drying of column will rapidly lower \nPWATs and bring drier conditions through the rest of the week and \ninto the weekend. However, given timing discrepancies in model and\nensemble guidance, lingering showers cannot be ruled out across \nthe Big Sandy as well as eastern portions of the Kentucky River \nand Upper Cumberland River basins on Thursday. Otherwise, \nseasonably cool weather takes hold: daytime highs in the 70s and \nnighttime lows in the 50s return from Wednesday night onward, with\nsome cooler valleys potentially dipping into the upper 40s.",
